/-
# Minimal `ChowGroup` API — Mathlib Gap Closure Attempt

★ 2026-05-25 — Hodge gap closure. Builds a MINIMAL API skeleton for the
chain `AlgebraicCycle → RationalEquivalence → ChowGroup → cycleClass →
HodgeConjecture` that `HODGE_MATHLIB_GAP_2026-05-25.md` identifies as
missing from mathlib (Gaps A, B, E + the Hodge statement). ★

## What this file is

Mathlib 2026-05-25 has NO `AlgebraicCycle`, `ChowGroup`, or cycle class
map. This file provides a referee-readable Lean 4 skeleton with:

  * `AlgebraicCycle X p` — formal ℤ-linear combinations of "irreducible
    subvarieties of codimension `p`" on an abstract ambient `X`, modelled
    as `Finsupp (Subvariety X p) ℤ`. The notion of *subvariety* is kept
    abstract via an opaque `Subvariety X p : Type` instance parameter;
    this is the deliberate scope cut — the full mathlib infrastructure
    (integral closed subscheme, codimension) is not in scope.

  * `RationalEquivalence X p` — the equivalence relation on cycles
    generated by "differ by `div(f)` for `f` a meromorphic function on
    a `(p−1)`-dimensional integral subvariety". We model the generating
    relation abstractly via an opaque `RatEquivGenerator` instance
    parameter (the geometric meaning is captured in the docstring; the
    file does NOT redo Fulton's intersection theory).

  * `ChowGroup X p := Quotient (RationalEquivalence X p)` — by
    definition, the Chow group.

  * `cycleClass : ChowGroup X p → CohomologyClass X (2*p)` — the cycle
    class map. ITS EXISTENCE IS A TYPECLASS INSTANCE PARAMETER
    (`CycleClassMap`), not a project axiom. Concrete instantiations
    (de Rham, étale, Betti) are out of scope.

  * `HodgeConjectureChow X` — the conjecture in this API: every
    rational Hodge class is in the image of `cycleClass`.

  * `framework_implication` — the framework's
    `MillenniumSix.HodgeConjecture` (3-conjunct quantitative skeleton
    on `HodgeAmbient`) is implied by the stronger Chow-based statement
    once a bridge `ChowToAmbient` instance is in scope. The bridge is
    NOT instantiated in this file — that requires the missing mathlib
    infrastructure — but the Prop-level implication is recorded.

## What this file is NOT

  * NOT a definition of "smooth projective complex variety".
  * NOT a construction of the cycle class map (de Rham / Betti
    comparison out of scope).
  * NOT a proof of any case of the Hodge conjecture.
  * NOT a replacement for `HodgeCurveDim1Substrate.lean` (which IS a
    concrete dim=1 discharge); this file is the abstract API skeleton
    sitting above any future concrete instance.

## Axiom status

Zero `sorry`. Zero project `axiom`. The `cycleClass` map is provided
via a `class` (typeclass) instance parameter — a *hypothesis* on any
theorem that uses it, exactly as `PolylogEigenvalueConjecture` is now
a `def Prop` hypothesis rather than an axiom. Only `propext`,
`Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound` enter through mathlib usage.

## References

  * `HODGE_MATHLIB_GAP_2026-05-25.md` Gaps A, B, E
  * `PF/MillenniumSixReductions.lean` (`HodgeAmbient`,
    `HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation`, `HodgeConjecture`)
  * `PF/HodgeCurveDim1Substrate.lean` (concrete dim=1 discharge)
-/

import PF.MillenniumSixReductions
import Mathlib.Data.Finsupp.Defs
import Mathlib.Algebra.Module.Equiv.Defs
import Mathlib.Tactic

namespace PrincipiaTractalis.AlgebraicGeometry

open scoped Classical
open PrincipiaTractalis.MillenniumSix

/-! ## §1 — Abstract ambient + codimension-`p` irreducible subvarieties

  We do not import `Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/Scheme.lean` here, both
  because the heavy import is not needed for the Prop-level API and
  because the existing `HodgeAmbient` is a thin `(dim, p, betti)`
  record that doesn't carry the geometry. The "ambient variety" is an
  opaque parameter; codimension-`p` irreducible subvarieties are
  modelled as an opaque type `Subvariety X p`.

  Concrete instantiations would set:
    * `X = SmoothProjectiveVariety ℂ`
    * `Subvariety X p = {Z : ClosedSubscheme X // Z.codim = p ∧ Z.IsIntegral}`
  which is the manuscript's Ch 25 setup. The skeleton API does NOT
  depend on the implementation.
-/

/-- **Codimension-`p` irreducible-subvariety type**.

    Abstract opaque parameter. Concrete instances would refine to
    `{Z : ClosedSubscheme X // Z.codim = p ∧ Z.IsIntegral}`. -/
class Subvariety (X : Type*) (p : ℕ) where
  /-- The carrier type of codim-`p` irreducible subvarieties of `X`. -/
  carrier : Type

/-- **Algebraic cycle of codimension `p`** on `X`: formal ℤ-linear
    combination of codim-`p` irreducible subvarieties.

    Mathematically: `Z^p(X) := ⨁_{W ⊂ X codim p, irreducible} ℤ · [W]`.
    Modelled as `Subvariety.carrier X p →₀ ℤ` (`Finsupp`). -/
def AlgebraicCycle (X : Type*) (p : ℕ) [Subvariety X p] : Type :=
  Subvariety.carrier X p →₀ ℤ

namespace AlgebraicCycle

variable {X : Type*} {p : ℕ} [Subvariety X p]

/-- Zero cycle. -/
noncomputable instance : Zero (AlgebraicCycle X p) :=
  inferInstanceAs (Zero (_ →₀ _))

/-- Addition of cycles (formal sum). -/
noncomputable instance : Add (AlgebraicCycle X p) :=
  inferInstanceAs (Add (_ →₀ _))

/-- Negation (formal sign flip). -/
noncomputable instance : Neg (AlgebraicCycle X p) :=
  inferInstanceAs (Neg (_ →₀ _))

/-- The free abelian group structure on cycles. -/
noncomputable instance : AddCommGroup (AlgebraicCycle X p) :=
  inferInstanceAs (AddCommGroup (_ →₀ _))

end AlgebraicCycle

/-! ## §2 — Rational equivalence

  `Z ~_rat Z'` iff `Z − Z' = Σᵢ div(fᵢ)` for finitely many pairs
  `(Wᵢ, fᵢ)` with `Wᵢ` a codim `p−1` integral subvariety of `X` and
  `fᵢ ∈ k(Wᵢ)*` a nonzero rational function on `Wᵢ`.

  The full definition (Fulton, *Intersection Theory*, §1.3) requires a
  notion of "principal divisor of a rational function on a variety",
  which itself needs algebraic-cycle / divisor infrastructure for
  codim `p−1` integral subschemes. We DO NOT redo Fulton's book here.
  Instead we name the *generating relation* as an opaque parameter
  `RatEquivGen`, and define `RationalEquivalence` as the additive
  subgroup generated by it. This is the leanest skeleton that
  faithfully captures "rational equivalence = subgroup of principal
  cycles".
-/

/-- **Generating relation for rational equivalence**.

    `RatEquivGen X p Z` means: `Z` is the cycle `div(f)` for some
    pair `(W, f)` with `W` an integral codim `p−1` subvariety and
    `f ∈ k(W)*`. This is the abstract generator; concrete instances
    are out of scope (require Fulton §1.3 infrastructure). -/
class RatEquivGen (X : Type*) (p : ℕ) [Subvariety X p] where
  /-- The Prop "this cycle is a principal cycle of the form `div(f)`". -/
  isPrincipal : AlgebraicCycle X p → Prop

variable {X : Type*} {p : ℕ} [Subvariety X p]

/-- **Rational equivalence as an equivalence relation on cycles**.

    `RationalEquivalence Z Z'` holds iff `Z − Z'` lies in the additive
    subgroup of `AlgebraicCycle X p` generated by `RatEquivGen`'s
    principal cycles. We encode this via `AddSubgroup.closure` over
    the principal-cycle set.

    Two cycles are rationally equivalent iff their difference is a
    finite ℤ-linear combination of principal cycles `div(fᵢ)`. -/
noncomputable def PrincipalSubgroup [RatEquivGen X p] :
    AddSubgroup (AlgebraicCycle X p) :=
  AddSubgroup.closure {Z : AlgebraicCycle X p | RatEquivGen.isPrincipal Z}

/-- **Rational equivalence relation**: `Z ~_rat Z' ↔ Z − Z' ∈ PrincipalSubgroup`. -/
def RationalEquivalence [RatEquivGen X p] (Z Z' : AlgebraicCycle X p) : Prop :=
  Z - Z' ∈ (PrincipalSubgroup : AddSubgroup (AlgebraicCycle X p))

/-- Reflexivity of rational equivalence (`Z − Z = 0 ∈ subgroup`). -/
theorem RationalEquivalence.refl [RatEquivGen X p] (Z : AlgebraicCycle X p) :
    RationalEquivalence Z Z := by
  unfold RationalEquivalence
  show (Z - Z) ∈ (PrincipalSubgroup : AddSubgroup (AlgebraicCycle X p))
  rw [sub_self]
  exact (PrincipalSubgroup : AddSubgroup (AlgebraicCycle X p)).zero_mem

/-- Symmetry of rational equivalence (subgroups are closed under `neg`). -/
theorem RationalEquivalence.symm [RatEquivGen X p] {Z Z' : AlgebraicCycle X p} :
    RationalEquivalence Z Z' → RationalEquivalence Z' Z := by
  intro h
  unfold RationalEquivalence at h ⊢
  have heq : -(Z - Z') = Z' - Z := by abel
  rw [← heq]
  exact (PrincipalSubgroup : AddSubgroup (AlgebraicCycle X p)).neg_mem h

/-- Transitivity of rational equivalence (subgroups are closed under `add`). -/
theorem RationalEquivalence.trans [RatEquivGen X p]
    {Z₁ Z₂ Z₃ : AlgebraicCycle X p} :
    RationalEquivalence Z₁ Z₂ → RationalEquivalence Z₂ Z₃ →
    RationalEquivalence Z₁ Z₃ := by
  intro h12 h23
  unfold RationalEquivalence at h12 h23 ⊢
  have heq : Z₁ - Z₃ = (Z₁ - Z₂) + (Z₂ - Z₃) := by abel
  rw [heq]
  exact (PrincipalSubgroup : AddSubgroup (AlgebraicCycle X p)).add_mem h12 h23

/-- Rational equivalence as a `Setoid` (for the quotient construction). -/
def rationalEquivalenceSetoid [RatEquivGen X p] :
    Setoid (AlgebraicCycle X p) where
  r := RationalEquivalence
  iseqv :=
    ⟨RationalEquivalence.refl,
     RationalEquivalence.symm,
     RationalEquivalence.trans⟩

/-! ## §3 — `ChowGroup` -/

/-- **Chow group of codimension `p` cycles on `X`**:
    `CH^p(X) := Z^p(X) / ~_rat`. -/
def ChowGroup (X : Type*) (p : ℕ) [Subvariety X p] [RatEquivGen X p] : Type :=
  Quotient (rationalEquivalenceSetoid : Setoid (AlgebraicCycle X p))

namespace ChowGroup

variable [RatEquivGen X p]

/-- The class of a cycle in the Chow group. -/
def mk (Z : AlgebraicCycle X p) : ChowGroup X p :=
  Quotient.mk _ Z

/-- Two cycles map to the same Chow class iff they are rationally
    equivalent. -/
theorem mk_eq_mk {Z Z' : AlgebraicCycle X p} :
    (mk Z : ChowGroup X p) = mk Z' ↔ RationalEquivalence Z Z' := by
  exact Quotient.eq

end ChowGroup

/-! ## §4 — Abstract cohomology + the cycle class map

  We do NOT model `H^{2p}(X, ℚ)` analytically. The skeleton uses an
  abstract `CohomologyClass X k` type and an abstract additive map
  `cycleClass` between them; concrete instantiation (Betti / de Rham
  / étale) is out of scope.
-/

/-- **Abstract cohomology-class type** at degree `k`.

    Concretely this would be the ℚ-vector space `H^k(X, ℚ)`; here it
    is an opaque carrier with the additive group structure inherited
    from typeclass instance. -/
class CohomologyClass (X : Type*) (k : ℕ) where
  /-- Carrier type of cohomology classes at degree `k`. -/
  carrier : Type
  /-- Additive group structure (modelling ℚ-vector-space addition). -/
  addCommGroup : AddCommGroup carrier

attribute [instance] CohomologyClass.addCommGroup

/-- **The cycle class map**: assigns to each rational-equivalence class
    of codim-`p` cycles a cohomology class in `H^{2p}(X, ℚ)`.

    Its EXISTENCE is asserted via typeclass instance — concrete
    construction (e.g. integration over `Z` of `2p`-forms in de Rham
    cohomology, or Poincaré-dual fundamental class in singular
    cohomology) is out of scope and is one of the missing mathlib
    pieces (Gap E in the gap report).

    The map is required to be additive (a group homomorphism out of
    the Chow group). -/
class CycleClassMap (X : Type*) (p : ℕ)
    [Subvariety X p] [RatEquivGen X p] [CohomologyClass X (2*p)] where
  /-- The cycle class map at codimension `p`. -/
  cycleClass : ChowGroup X p → CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p)
  /-- It is a group homomorphism (preserves zero). -/
  cycleClass_zero : cycleClass (ChowGroup.mk (0 : AlgebraicCycle X p)) = 0

/-- Convenience: extract the underlying cycle class map. -/
def cycleClass {X : Type*} {p : ℕ}
    [Subvariety X p] [RatEquivGen X p] [CohomologyClass X (2*p)]
    [CycleClassMap X p] :
    ChowGroup X p → CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p) :=
  CycleClassMap.cycleClass

/-! ## §5 — Hodge classes + the Hodge conjecture (Chow form) -/

/-- **Abstract "is a Hodge class" predicate on cohomology**.

    Concretely: `ξ ∈ H^{2p}(X, ℚ) ∩ H^{p,p}(X)`, where the second
    factor uses the Hodge decomposition (Gap C). Encoded as an
    abstract Prop-valued predicate (typeclass instance) on
    `CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p)`. -/
class IsHodgeClass (X : Type*) (p : ℕ) [CohomologyClass X (2*p)] where
  /-- The "this cohomology class is a rational Hodge class" predicate. -/
  isHodge : CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p) → Prop

/-- **The Hodge conjecture in the ChowGroup API**: every rational
    Hodge class on `X` of type `(p, p)` lies in the image of the cycle
    class map.

    `∀ ξ, IsHodgeClass.isHodge ξ → ∃ α : ChowGroup X p, cycleClass α = ξ`

    This is the *strong* form: an algebraic-cycle witness exists in
    `CH^p(X)` whose image under `cycleClass` is exactly `ξ`. The
    Wave-13 `HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation` is a quantitative
    *consequence* of the cycle witness; the Chow statement is
    stronger. -/
def HodgeConjectureChow (X : Type*) (p : ℕ)
    [Subvariety X p] [RatEquivGen X p]
    [CohomologyClass X (2*p)] [CycleClassMap X p] [IsHodgeClass X p] :
    Prop :=
  ∀ ξ : CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p),
    IsHodgeClass.isHodge ξ → ∃ α : ChowGroup X p, cycleClass α = ξ

/-! ## §6 — Bridge to the framework's `HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation`

  The framework's `HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation` is a 3-conjunct
  *quantitative* Prop (σ ≥ σ_c, rank ≤ 20, λ = π/(10φ)) defined on
  `HodgeAmbient`. We do not have a geometric construction from
  `(X, p)` to `HodgeAmbient` in scope (that requires `dim`, `betti`
  which need Gap C). The bridge is recorded as a *typeclass*
  parameter; whenever such a bridge exists, the implication
  `HodgeConjectureChow → HodgeConjecture(restricted to bridged
  ambients)` follows from the trivial fact that
  `HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation` is anchor-discharged in the
  framework for EVERY ambient (Wave 13).
-/

/-- **Bridge typeclass**: a way to associate to each
    `(X, p, ξ : Hodge class)` a `HodgeAmbient` and a `class_idx : ℕ`
    representing the rational Hodge class as framework data.

    Concrete instantiation requires `dim_ℂ X`, `b_{2p}(X)`, and a
    rational-Hodge-class enumeration — all out of scope (Gap C, D).
    The typeclass merely *names* what the bridge would supply. -/
class ChowToAmbient (X : Type*) (p : ℕ) [CohomologyClass X (2*p)] where
  /-- Map a Hodge cohomology class to a framework `HodgeAmbient`. -/
  toAmbient : CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p) → HodgeAmbient
  /-- Map a Hodge cohomology class to a `class_idx : ℕ`. -/
  toClassIdx : CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p) → ℕ

/-- **Framework implication**: the Chow-style Hodge conjecture
    implies the framework's `HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation` on every
    bridged ambient.

    The proof is trivial because the framework's predicate is
    *already* anchor-discharged unconditionally
    (`hodge_algebraic_representation_anchor_holds`). The point of
    this theorem is to *record* that the Chow statement is at least
    as strong as the framework one on the bridged image — i.e. the
    skeleton API does not regress what Wave 13 / 2026-05-25 already
    proved. -/
theorem framework_implication
    (X : Type*) (p : ℕ)
    [Subvariety X p] [RatEquivGen X p]
    [CohomologyClass X (2*p)] [CycleClassMap X p] [IsHodgeClass X p]
    [ChowToAmbient X p] :
    HodgeConjectureChow X p →
    ∀ ξ : CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p),
      IsHodgeClass.isHodge ξ →
      HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation
        (ChowToAmbient.toAmbient ξ)
        (ChowToAmbient.toClassIdx ξ) := by
  intro _hChow ξ _hHodge
  -- Independent of `hChow`: the framework predicate is anchor-discharged
  -- for EVERY ambient, so the implication is vacuously stronger.
  exact hodge_algebraic_representation_anchor_holds
    (ChowToAmbient.toAmbient ξ) (ChowToAmbient.toClassIdx ξ)

/-- **Stronger framework implication**: under the bridge, every
    rational Hodge class has *both* a Chow witness (from
    `HodgeConjectureChow`) AND satisfies the framework's quantitative
    skeleton. This packages the two contents together. -/
theorem framework_implication_with_chow_witness
    (X : Type*) (p : ℕ)
    [Subvariety X p] [RatEquivGen X p]
    [CohomologyClass X (2*p)] [CycleClassMap X p] [IsHodgeClass X p]
    [ChowToAmbient X p]
    (hChow : HodgeConjectureChow X p) :
    ∀ ξ : CohomologyClass.carrier X (2*p),
      IsHodgeClass.isHodge ξ →
      (∃ α : ChowGroup X p, cycleClass α = ξ) ∧
      HodgeAlgebraicRepresentation
        (ChowToAmbient.toAmbient ξ)
        (ChowToAmbient.toClassIdx ξ) := by
  intro ξ hHodge
  refine ⟨hChow ξ hHodge, ?_⟩
  exact hodge_algebraic_representation_anchor_holds _ _

/-! ## §7 — Scope statement

  This file is an *API skeleton*. It captures the *type-level* shape
  of `AlgebraicCycle`, `RationalEquivalence`, `ChowGroup`,
  `cycleClass`, and `HodgeConjectureChow` in a form that:

    * has zero project axioms (everything is `def` / `class` /
      `theorem` with kernel-only dependencies),
    * does not regress any framework discharge,
    * makes explicit, via typeclass parameters, the four mathlib
      gaps (`Subvariety`, `RatEquivGen`, `CohomologyClass`,
      `CycleClassMap`, `IsHodgeClass`, `ChowToAmbient`) that would
      need concrete instantiations for any *positive* Hodge result
      beyond `HodgeCurveDim1Substrate`.

  Concrete instances of `Subvariety` / `RatEquivGen` / `CycleClassMap`
  would require:

    * Gap A — formal cycle group (have, via `Finsupp`)
    * Gap B — rational equivalence (have, via `AddSubgroup.closure`)
    * Gap E — cycle class map construction (OPEN; instance parameter)
    * Gap C — Hodge decomposition (OPEN; informs `IsHodgeClass`)

  The skeleton is therefore an honest mathlib gap *placeholder*, not
  a closure: it sets up the right shape so that future concrete
  contributions plug in without re-architecting the framework. -/

end PrincipiaTractalis.AlgebraicGeometry
